Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||11-27-2000||11:16 PM||||the one where you vote for the actual presidential candidate.. the non-electoral vote thing.<P>isn't that the term for it? I'm sorry if I got it wrong, there's still a bit I still don't know about the american political system..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cheever2||11-27-2000||11:27 PM||||Are you saying you have to vote for one PARTY or another?  You don't vote for a specific person?  Bummer.  <P>The electoral thing is regarding how the votes get counted.  The guy who gets the most actual votes in a state gets the "points" for winning that state (e.g. win Florida, get 25 points).  But after adding up all the states + D.C. etc, it sometimes happens that you got the most REAL votes but got fewer ELECTORAL votes (what I called points).  <P>But the person's NAME is on the ballot, and that's what we select REGARDLESS of what party they are or what party the voter is.  The only thing we can't choose is their Vice Presidential running mate.  <P>Did I read you right?  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||DaRkLoRD||11-28-2000||12:10 AM||||We vote for the candidate in our district, and not for the actual leader of the country.. and if there's no one from your party running in the electoral district where you live, you have no way of voting for that party. that's the problem I encountered today.. I liked how on the Florida ballots (which I didn't find confusing at all) the presidential candidate's names were printed, and not just a local person.<P>(I mentioned the florida ones, because they were the only US ballots I've seen...)<BR>------------------<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>   <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 11-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfixit58||11-28-2000||10:20 AM||||Just a little note on "popular" versus "elector" vote.<P>The United Stated is a Republic not a Democratic nation. Our founding fathers believed that each state should get an equal vote proportionally to its population. Hence, Florida gets 25 elector votes, Wyoming gets 3 (the minimum number of votes). What this does is give each state a fare say-so about who represents the country as president.<P>If it we, as a country, elected our president strictly on the popular vote, the smaller states would have little to no effect on the outcome of the election because there population was is too small.<P>The elector college is a good system. It's only because Gore didn't win that it is an issue this year. This year, the smaller states (the heartland of America) decided to vote for Bush. Their vote DID count!<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tigger||11-28-2000||10:48 AM||||Yes, but on the day of the election, I remember a poll that showed most people were afraid Gore would win the electorial vote, and Bush would win the popular vote.  There was a real good chance that this would happen, and many people wanted to scrap the electoral college because of this.<P>Now that it pretty much looks like there is a good chance it will go the other way around, you don't hear the Bush supporters whining and complaining about it so much anymore...<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 11-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||loafer87gt||11-28-2000||12:44 PM||||At least the Canadian Alliance swept all the western provinces. I think it's kinda stupid that Ontario has so many damn seats, basically whoever the popular vote there is is guaranteed to be the next leading party. I just can't believe the liberals got such a strong majority again though, unbelievable!||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfixit58||11-28-2000||05:32 PM||||You won't hear me complaining about the Elector College. I think it's a good system. Gore keeps throwing out that he won the popular vote. Really... it could just as easily have gone either way... just like the Florida vote. Fact is, this country is split and Gore is trying to drive the wedge even deeper. Kind of like a spoiled child... If I can't have it then, NOBODY ELSE CAN either.<P>It's just hard for me to believe that, even after all the lies he and his group keep spreading, even as some of the most liberal news papers and networks are starting to point out these lies, that there are STILL people who would like him to represent our country.<P>He says he wants "every vote counted" -- but he sues to keep the absentee military votes (which favor Bush) from the total.<P>He claims he wants a "fair and accurate count" -- but he only petitioned to have the heavily democrate counties re-counted.<P>He claims he wants to "follow the law" -- yet he challenges every law in the state of Florida and is now trying to challenge the law of the Federal Government.<P>He claims to be a proponent of the womens sufferage movoment -- yet he allows his goons to belittle Ms. Harris for following the LAW.<P>Oh yea... he invented the internet, too. LOL<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||DaRkLoRD||11-28-2000||06:21 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfixit58:<BR><B>Just a little note on "popular" versus "elector" vote.<P>The United Stated is a Republic not a Democratic nation. Our founding fathers believed that each state should get an equal vote proportionally to its population. Hence, Florida gets 25 elector votes, Wyoming gets 3 (the minimum number of votes). What this does is give each state a fare say-so about who represents the country as president.<P>If it we, as a country, elected our president strictly on the popular vote, the smaller states would have little to no effect on the outcome of the election because there population was is too small.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The problem with this, is the individual states are being treated as separate entities, instead of polling the entire population as a whole. If the whole country was polled exactly the same, and no regard was given to what state the voter was in, it might be more fair. It wouldn't matter if a voter in Florida or Wyoming voted for Bush, their individual vote would count the same, regardless of how big their state's population was.<P>------------------<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>   <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 11-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cheever2||11-28-2000||07:10 PM||||What I don't like is that my vote only counts as part of my little group (state), and the electoral thing says "most of us in this state voted this way, so we're <B>all</B> voting this way".  I want my vote to truly be worth one vote, and I want it to be part of the BIG picture, not a piece of the picture.<P>I didn't get registered in my new location in time, and couldn't drive out to where I was still registered, so I didn't vote (not that I care anymore, so flame me).  But because the electoral vote for my state went the way I preferred, it didn't matter anyway.  I don't like the thought that my vote would not have mattered.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||11-28-2000||08:35 PM||||The electoral mess gives small states a voice? How do you figure. The only reason candidates come to DE at all is to payback big contributors and make it look good. If we weren't just a few miles from philly and baltimore they wouldn't come here even then if they could avoid it.<P>As for the paper ballot. those things should have been banned years ago. This isn't the first time these things have been trouble. paper ballots have always been subject to fraud corruption and other things... <P>The reason these thing still exist is cost. The old pull a lever machine was expencive and required an artist to service them. The smallest thing could jam the hell out of one. <P>The new computer machine we use here in DE is next to imposible to screw up. The ballot these new machines accept is huge, biger than most news papers completely open. (D size paper for you drafting guys.) To vote you push the X and a light comes on. To change your mind hit the X again to reset the catagory and then push a diferant X. Whey you are done you hit the big green button on the bottom.||||reg||1
Z||000014||olympic||11-29-2000||03:14 AM||||A quick run down on the canadian election system:<P>   To win a majority gov't, a party has to win atleast 151 out of the 301 seats in the parliament.  Each of the 301 seats represents a constituency which are regions of Canada that are suppose to have roughly the same population in each one.  Each constituency will have candidates from the different parties and these are the people you vote for.  The candidate who gets the most votes in his constituency wins that seat and become a member of parliament.<P>   Each party has a leader and this is the person who will become the Prime Minister if that party wins the election.  Since we have more than 2 parties running it is possible that one party will not get the required 151 seats to form a majority gov't.  3 things can happen here.  The winning party can join with another party and combine their seats to get a majority.(This just happened here in the last provincial election)  Or the 2 losing parties can join to overthrow the winning party and force another election.  Or the winning party can try to function as a minority gov't but that isn't likely.<P>  So you can see that this isn't too far off from the US electoral college system.  Instead of getting X number of electoral votes for winning a state we get X number of members of parliament.  What is p*ssing us western canadians off is that ontario and quebec basically decide the outcome because they have most of Canada's population.  Those 2 provinces alone represent nearly 200 seats out of 301.  The current liberal gov't has basically told western canada to shove it and there is nothing we can do about it.||||reg||1
Z||000015||mrfixit58||11-29-2000||11:03 AM||||Darklord -- Yes. Each state IS treated as a seperate entity. As they should be. The interests of Iowa are different then those of New Jersey. Florida is different then Maine. And EVERYBODY is different from California. That's the point.<P>If the USA was a Democracy instead of a Republic, then the popular vote would rule. If that was the case, then just have the states with the highest population vote and be done with it. Heck, don't bother the whole state, just have the most highly populated cities vote. That way all of you who live in rural America, rasing most of the food and natural materials for the WHOLE country won't be bothered. Just stay on your tractor and trust those people on welfare to vote your best interest. Never mind that they won't contribute to society since they don't pay taxes on welfare.<P>Ogre -- Yes, it sucks when you vote one way and your state gives its Electoral vote to the the other candidate. Heavy unionized states will always vote democrate (why, I don't know -- under Gore's plan most union employees make too much to qualify for ANY tax deduction). But, having the college keeps a balance of power between to polorized parties.<P>As far as the punch cards. It's funny... you didn't hear any complaints from the Democrates when Clinton won -- TWICE. Florida is not the only state to use them. As far as the butterfly ballots goes -- Both Jessie Jackson and Dailey use EXACTLY the same kind of ballot in their home districts. They must have really SMART voters there. I guess the people of Palm Beach county (by the way the richest county in all of AMERICA) are just too STUPID to read.<P>Did you know that, nation wide, there are over 2,000,000 undervotes?!? And Gore wants to base the entire election on approximately 14,000 votes in only heavely Democrate counties?<P>Here is a verble picture for ya... Suppose you have fifty fruit trees on one side of the road and only one of the same type of tree on the other. After harvesting ALL fifty-one trees you tell the workers to go back to all the trees and make sure you didn't miss any fruit. Now, which side of the road do you think will find more fruit?? The obvious answer is the side with the most trees, right? Well, the counties that Gore wants re-counted are 50:1 and 70:1 Democrate to Republican. Now... who really wants a "fair and accurate count"? How bogus.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||frontal lobe||11-29-2000||11:50 AM||||The USA is supposed to be a united group of states.  That is how our country started out.  The states are supposed to retain some autonomy on how they want to live/run things, etc.  Then if you don't like how one state does things, you are free to move to another state that does.  However, over the years the FEDERAL government has mandated more and more to the individual states.  I understand this to some extent, as travel and communication has minimized the differences between the country.  That said, I would still like the constitution to be followed more and allow states rights, instead of what liberal activist judges have done in bastardizing the constitution and usurping control for the federal government that was never intended.<P>Anyway, we are a republic still at this point, and the individual STATES elect the president, not the general populace.  It can be debated both ways.||||reg||1
Z||000017||mrfixit58||11-29-2000||03:05 PM||||Lobe -- AMEN!||||reg||1
